# Create doc

When the user applies **create-doc** to a folder of tutorial videos, write one contents file and one chapter per video. Read `Personal/Files/azure/sample.md` once. Copy its section order, accordion markup, and spacing. Do not copy its sentences, and do not rewrite `sample.md`. Only run this workflow when the user asks to apply the rule.

## Which folders

The user names three places:

1. The video folder. Every `mp4`, `mkv`, `mov`, and `webm` in that folder is one chapter. Nested files count. Example: `Personal/course_videos/Course AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator course with SIMULATIONS`.
2. The folder, or the file path, for the contents file.
3. The folder for the chapter files.

If either output location is missing, ask before writing. A named file is the contents path. A named folder gets `{slug}.md` inside it: the exam code in the course title when it has one (`az-104.md`), otherwise the kebab-case course name.

## Contents file

The contents file is the index. It does not hold the lessons.

The first line is a back link. Use the parent page the user names. When they do not name one and the file is under `Personal/Files/azure/`, link `azure_ai.md` with the relative path from the contents file, labeled `Back to AZURE & AI`.

Then a `#` heading with the course title, then `## Table of Contents`, then one link per video. Sort by the number at the start of the filename. `2` comes before `10`. Keep gaps. The link text matches that chapter's `#` heading. The target is the chapter file, relative to the contents file. Paths below show the shape; compute the real relative path from the folders the user named.

```markdown
[Back to AZURE & AI](../../azure_ai.md)

# AZ-104: Microsoft Azure Administrator

## Table of Contents

- [Chapter 45 — Understanding storage accounts](md/045-understanding-storage-accounts.md)
- [Chapter 46 — Create and configure storage accounts](md/046-create-and-configure-storage-accounts.md)
```

When the contents file already exists, add links that are missing. Leave every other line as it is.

## One chapter per video

Write in numeric order. Skip a video whose chapter file is already there, unless the user asks to redo it. A range in the request limits which videos you write. Add that chapter's contents link as soon as the chapter file exists.

Chapter filename: `{nnn}-{kebab-title}.md` in the chapter folder. `nnn` is the filename number, zero-padded to the width of the highest number, and at least three digits. The title is the filename after that number, without the extension.

The chapter follows `sample.md`, then one more section:

1. Back link to the contents file: `[Back to {course title}](relative-path)`.
2. `# Chapter N — Title` with an em dash. N is the filename number, not padded. The title is the video title.
3. `## Table of Contents` linking Introduction, Detailed tutorial, each tutorial accordion, Summary, References, and Questions and Answers. Nest tutorial accordions under Detailed tutorial. Nest each question under Questions and Answers.
4. `## Introduction` — a few sentences on what this video teaches and why it matters.
5. `## Detailed tutorial` — one bold source line, then one `<details>` accordion per idea.
6. `## Summary` — short bullets of the facts a reader should remember.
7. `## References` — the video, then official docs for topics the video names. For Azure, Microsoft Learn. Do not invent URLs.
8. `## Questions and Answers` — 10 to 20 questions from this video. The answer is inside that question's accordion.

The source line names the course and links the video. URL-encode spaces. Link a transcript only when you wrote one beside the video.

```markdown
**AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator** · [46 - Create and configure storage accounts.mp4](../../course_videos/Course%20AZ-104%20Microsoft%20Azure%20Administrator%20course%20with%20SIMULATIONS/46%20-%20Create%20and%20configure%20storage%20accounts.mp4)
```

## Watch the video before writing

Transcribe the audio and read the frames before writing the chapter. A chapter written from the filename is not done. If transcription fails, stop that video and say so. Do not fill the gap from the title.

Blades, commands, settings, and numbers come from the video. When a caption disagrees with the screen, follow the screen and say so.

Save a frame when the prose refers to a blade, diagram, command, or result. Skip a frame that only shows a talking head. Put frames in `figures/` next to the chapter files, named `{nnn}-{mm}-{ss}.jpg`. From the chapter, link `figures/046-12-08.jpg`. Meaningful alt text, then an italic caption:

```markdown
![12:08 — Storage account redundancy blade with RA-GRS selected](figures/046-12-08.jpg)

*Figure: 12:08. Redundancy is set to read-access geo-redundant storage.*
```

## Accordions

One accordion is one idea. A simulation step that changes one setting is one idea. A sentence is not.

```markdown
<details id="what-ra-grs-keeps">
<summary><strong>What RA-GRS keeps</strong></summary>

Read-access geo-redundant storage copies the account to the paired region and lets you read that copy.

</details>
```

- `id` is the kebab-case slug used in the table of contents.
- `<summary>` is sentence case, wrapped in `<strong>`, and names the idea.
- One blank line after `</summary>` and one blank line before `</details>`.
- Keep definitions, examples, numbers, and the names of blades and settings.
- Turn comparisons into Markdown tables.

## Questions and Answers

This section comes after References. Use ten questions for a short video and up to twenty when it covers many settings or a long simulation. Stay inside 10–20. Every question is distinct and answerable from this video alone. Do not import questions from another video or from the practice-exam files. The summary does not give the answer.

```markdown
<details id="question-1-which-redundancy-is-readable">
<summary><strong>Question 1 — Which redundancy is readable</strong></summary>

Which redundancy option copies the storage account to the paired region and lets you read the copy before a failover?

**Answer.** Read-access geo-redundant storage (RA-GRS). Geo-redundant storage copies to the paired region, but that copy stays unreadable until the account fails over.

</details>
```

- `id` is `question-N-kebab-title`.
- Summary: `Question N — short title`, wrapped in `<strong>`.
- The body asks the question, then **Answer** states the correct choice in words and one short reason.
- Same blank-line spacing as the other accordions.
- The chapter table of contents links each question under Questions and Answers.

## Voice

Write the way the sample is written: short paragraphs, plain sentences, tables when a comparison is the point. Keep the product's own terms. Do not add greetings, and do not leave player chrome in the body.
